            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Study: $500 Spent, Zero Conversions—Where Did the Optimizer Go Wrong?</h2>
<p>A utility app advertising team discovered during their weekly creative review that a video creative had consumed $532 but showed zero conversions in the backend. The optimizer's first reaction: maybe the targeting was too narrow? Should they broaden the audience?</p>
<p>But looking closer at the backend data, this creative had a CTR of 3.2%, above category average; completion rate was 68%, not bad either. It was clearly "running"—impressions and engagement were there. But why no conversions?</p>
<p>This scenario plays out in countless ad accounts daily. The gap between "running" and "selling" isn't about budget—it's about evaluation dimensions.</p>
<h2>Three Signals to Identify "Runs But Doesn't Sell" Creatives</h2>
<p><strong>Signal 1: CTR Above Average, But CPA Keeps Climbing</strong></p>
<p>High click-through means the hook worked. But if users take no action after clicking, or CPA increases daily, the landing page and product proposition aren't aligned. Users are attracted by the ad but can't find what they came for.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 2: Short Creative Lifespan, Rapid Decay After Initial Success</strong></p>
<p>These creatives often rely on novelty or emotional刺激 to spike early metrics, but users stop responding after the first view. The spend curve shows "sharp rise, sharp fall"—total spend stays low while cost per order climbs higher.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 3: Account Is Spending, But ROI Remains Negative</strong></p>
<p>If multiple creatives are running and the account shows spend scale but ROI never turns positive, the issue isn't about optimizing individual creatives. The fundamental value proposition in the creatives may not be viable.</p>
<h2>Four-Step Review Method: Turn "Running" Into "Selling"</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Map the Conversion Path, Find the Breakage Point</strong></p>
<p>Not all post-click drop-off is a landing page problem. First check what users did on-site—did they bounce immediately, browse product pages but not add to cart, or add to cart but not purchase? Different breakage points require different optimization approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High bounce rate</strong> → Check landing page load speed and copy relevance</li>
<li><strong>High page UV but low add-to-cart rate</strong> → Check if product images and videos showcase core selling points</li>
<li><strong>High add-to-cart but low checkout rate</strong> → Check price competitiveness and checkout flow smoothness</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 2: Distinguish Organic Traffic from Paid Traffic</strong></p>
<p>If the same creative performs well in organic posts but converts poorly in paid promotion, the creative likely adapted to users'主动 browsing behavior but failed to adapt to ads' passive reach logic. Ad creatives need to deliver core value within 3 seconds, rather than assuming users will explore proactively.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Calculate the Creative's True Contribution, Not Just Surface Spend</strong></p>
<p>High spend doesn't equal high contribution. Break creative contribution into two parts: direct conversion contribution and indirect conversion contribution. Indirect conversions occur when users saw the ad but didn't purchase immediately, then completed the purchase through organic channels within a certain timeframe. This is common in products with longer decision cycles but often overlooked by optimizers.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Establish Creative "Life and Death" Standards</strong></p>
<p>Set clear decision thresholds for creatives at different stages:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Under $100 spend</strong>: Focus on CTR; if CTR is below 1.5%, pause immediately</li>
<li><strong>$100-500 spend</strong>: Start tracking CPA; if CPA exceeds 150% of target, pause for observation</li>
<li><strong>Over $500 spend</strong>: If ROI still negative, keep for observation but don't increase budget; test new creatives to replace</li>
</ul>
<h2>Final Thoughts: Running Is the Basic Threshold, Selling Is the Goal**</h2>
<p>Creatives that can run are recognized by the system and have opportunities to reach target users. But impressions don't equal conversions, and attention doesn't equal purchase intent. During reviews, ask one more question: "What did these clicks bring?" rather than "How much budget does this creative have left?"</p>
<p>The prerequisite for selling is that the creative truly solves a problem users are willing to pay for. If reach is good but conversions are zero, don't rush to adjust targeting. Start by checking the product value proposition and landing page experience—this is often more effective than adjusting bids.</p>
